## 2 types of ChatGPT users

0:00 You might’ve seen Google, Amazon, and Shopify say that AI usage at work is

0:05 now a baseline expectation, and everyone is scrambling to futureproof themselves

0:10 and keep up with the exponential change happening every single day.

0:14 And we all face the problem of: "Okay, I’ve learned this, but now

0:17 ChatGPT rolls out a new model—and everything I learned can now be done with a click of a button."

0:22 So how do I keep up?

0:24 How do I know which tools are useful and which ones are just gimmicks?

0:29 As a framework thinker—because you're on this side

0:31 of the internet—we’re looking for that 80/20 way of understanding how we can

0:36 incorporate AI into our knowledge work so we stay sane and stand out at work.

0:41 It’s not about fancy syntax or the symbols you have to use.

0:44 (Though if you want the basics, here they are—take a screenshot.)

0:47 It’s also not about:

0:49 "Do I use ChatGPT or Claude? Which one’s better? Should I use both? Should I use all of them?"

0:54 Those things don’t matter.

0:56 What matters is how you use AI to help you improve the signal among all the noise.

1:03 Where people get tripped up is this fake notion that AI is just

1:07 about efficiency—so everything needs to be quick.

1:10 Here’s what I mean:

1:11 We currently see two types of people when it comes to using LLMs.

1:15 There are those who put 10 words in and get 1,000 words out.

1:18 They think that’s an efficient use of their time—things come out in seconds,

1:23 and they can move on to the next task.

1:24 Then there are those who put 1,000 words in and get 1,000 words back.

1:28 On the surface, that might not sound great. You might think, “Well, I could’ve just

1:31 written those 1,000 words myself.” But the result you get from ChatGPT

1:36 can be of much higher quality than what you can write on your own in the same time.

1:42 Most knowledge workers aren’t using AI this way.

## A knowledge worker's leverage

1:45 People over-obsess about the concept of time. “I have this task—I need to get it done.”

1:51 But as a knowledge worker, your leverage doesn’t come from time.

1:54 You're not being paid by the hour. Your value is in the quality of

1:59 the idea, and how far you can take something that others can’t even see.

2:03 The fundamental of any company comes down to differentiation.

2:07 How does your company differentiate from competitors?

2:09 How do you, as an employee, differentiate yourself from someone with similar skills?

2:13 So don’t think about time—think about quality of output.

2:18 I also talked about this in another video, where I said:

2:21 You can use AI to increase the quality of your input

2:24 in order to improve the quality of your output.

2:27 99% of people are stuck in this 10-words-in, 1,000-words-out level.

2:33 You see two extremes:

2:35 Experts who really know their stuff—and when they use ChatGPT, they go,

2:40 “The quality of this is terrible. It’s a waste of my time. I might as well write it myself.”

2:46 But they’re not using ChatGPT the right way.

2:49 Then there’s the other side of the coin: “I don’t care about the quality. Let me

2:54 put in 10 words, get 1,000 back, check the box, and move on to the next thing.”

2:58 Neither is a long-term strategy, especially if you're a knowledge worker.

## ChatGPT is not a source for answer

3:04 Most people look at ChatGPT as a source for answers,

3:09 but what the top 1% do is see it as a way to refine the problem.

3:17 Einstein famously said: “If I had an hour to work

3:19 on a problem, I’d spend 55 minutes on the problem and 5 minutes on the solution.”

3:24 Most of us don't do that, we do exactly the opposite.

3:27 I recently did a guest lecture at a university.

3:31 I saw how smart these students are—fast learners, great ideas, very articulate.

3:40 The professor brought me in to talk about mental models, precisely because the students

3:44 are excellent at solving problems —but they don’t have experience coming up with problems,

3:51 identifying or reframing them to get to the right answer.

3:57 We see this at work too.

3:59 The boss gives us the problem, and we jump in to solve it without thinking:

4:05 “Wait, is this the right problem?”

4:07 Most employees are good students.

4:09 Now that AI is here, how you stand out as a knowledge worker

4:13 is not in how quickly you can give a good answer—

4:17 but in how well you can define, reframe, and align everyone on the right problem.

4:24 This is such good news.

4:26 Because AI doesn’t have your experience, judgment, or intuition—

4:32 all of which you've built over years.

4:34 So knowing where the problem is , how to phrase it,

4:37 and how to extract value from AI tools like ChatGPT is key.

